 17592186569697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569898 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569898 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569898 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569898 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186569845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569898 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8f97bdd2-bf1f-57d2-a993-56bdac1ca568" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569943 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569943 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569943 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569943 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569943 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569943 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569943 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569943 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186569691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569943 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e8f8080f-0b51-545f-9d71-93e2c857b6d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569984 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569984 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569984 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569984 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,2,\\,…$\n\nII. ${a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3}{2}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=-1,\\,n\\ge 1$\n\nIII. $1,\\,0.2,\\,0.03,\\,…$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569984 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42a89ddb-b42b-5afd-9218-01f794cbc72e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569984 #edb/ref 1074 "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not a geometric sequence because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is a geometric sequence. The recursive rule ${a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3}{2}{a}_{n}$ tells us that each term is $\\frac{3}{2}$ times the previous term, so the common ratio is $r=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- Sequence III is not a geometric sequence because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{0.2}{1} & =0.2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{0.03}{0.2} & =0.15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence II is a geometric sequence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570006 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the $4$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-6{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{36},\\,n\\ge 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "857b63a1-09cd-5ba9-9404-4c37877c9864" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570006 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =-6{a}_{1}=-6(\\frac{1}{36})=-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-6{a}_{2}=-6(-\\frac{1}{6})=1 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =-6{a}_{3}=-6(1)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${a}_{4}=-6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570036 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570036 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570036 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570036 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the $5$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=-\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=80,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570036 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5baa90b-efb9-5b0e-bee8-5a62fcaafd24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570036 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $5$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =80 \\\\ f(2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}f(1)=-\\frac{1}{2}(80)=-40 \\\\ f(3) & =-\\frac{1}{2}f(2)=-\\frac{1}{2}(-40)=20 \\\\ f(4) & =-\\frac{1}{2}f(3)=-\\frac{1}{2}(20)=-10 \\\\ f(5) & =-\\frac{1}{2}f(4)=-\\frac{1}{2}(-10)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(5)=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570069 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186570075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570069 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6eaf6155-fd31-5612-a495-df002400eda9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570069 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186570036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570075 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186570093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570075 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "90cd1598-b2f8-5d28-8110-090cd43a789d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570075 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186569984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570093 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d7c48d56-9ae9-568b-9b4a-0e104f0a6f6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570093 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186570006 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
